#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""scope-pass — the parts of a scope's weekly pass that code does (knowledge-layers S5). Stdlib, 3.9.

    scope-pass where SCOPE [--day D]    what the pass reads and writes, one `<name> <value>` line each,
                                        and `ready yes|no — why`; exit 1 when not ready, 2 no such scope
    scope-pass check SCOPE [FILE] [--judge] [--json]
                                        exit 1, naming each line, where FILE (default: the pass's draft)
                                        names a confidential scope's repos, paths or words; --judge: a
                                        clean scan then goes to a judge (the draft: record's `scope.upstream`; another FILE: `scope.brief`), exit 1
                                        on its `leak`; 2 when either cannot tell; --json: one verdict
                                        object on stdout, the lines on stderr
    scope-pass record SCOPE [--day D]   land the pass: the dream on the anchor's records branch, pushed, its
                                        pull request opened and made ready; the draft, scanned and
                                        (confidential) judged, as the vault's inbox file with one log.md line
                                        (a word: complete or held), by knowledge-commit. Exit 0 recorded,
                                        3 held (rewrite the draft), 4 LEAKED, 1 could not finish (a judge that
                                        could not tell, a commit, a push: run it again), 2 a usage error or a
                                        word list that cannot judge (a `re:` line, the scan's clock)
    scope-pass receipt SCOPE [--day D]  the day's receipt, verified against the vault's file, as JSON: exit 0
                                        certified, 3 held, 1 otherwise (weekly-run logs it; the barrier reads it)
    scope-pass proof SCOPE [--day D]    exit 0 LAUNCHED when the dream is on the anchor's main and the inbox
                                        file, certified, on the vault's main; 5 WAITING when the dream is
                                        pushed on its records branch with a ready pull request (the anchor's
                                        merge lands it) — no origin, no GitHub, no token or no ready PR is
                                        MISSING, never WAITING; 3 HELD when record held the draft back; 4 LEAKED
                                        when the inbox file on the vault's main names the scope, or a
                                        confidential one's bytes match no complete receipt of the day; 2 when
                                        the node's word list cannot judge and the receipts settle no LAUNCHED,
                                        WAITING or LEAKED (record would refuse the same list; a HELD reads so too); 1 otherwise — a missing or stale
                                        receipt is never LAUNCHED

**The pass** (`/aurora dream --scope <scope>`, sections/dream-scope.md) is the global dream bounded
to one scope of the registry: its Gather reads that scope alone (S4's `--scope` readers), and it
writes two files, both in the node: the scope's dream, `<anchor>/dreams/<day>.md`, which stays there
and may name anything in it, and the draft of its inbox file, `<anchor>/dreams/<day>-upstream.md`.
The inbox file, `<vault>/inbox/upstream/<scope>.md`, is the only thing that leaves, and only record
writes it, from the draft, once check passes: the vault's SessionEnd hook commits and pushes whatever
lies in its in_place paths, so unchecked text written there would reach GitHub before any check ran
(S5 landing review, 2026-10-22). It holds generalised lines the global dream reads as inbox (the design's D2-A: "nothing reads down into a
confidential node; only generalised knowledge flows upstream"). The anchor is the scope's first repo,
named, never a glob — its knowledge repo, `<scope>-knowledge` by convention (atlas's `knowledge-home`
reads it the same way). The dream reaches GitHub on the anchor's **records branch**,
`claude/<host>/<day>-scope-pass` (kl-s5 D1-B, Evolve S2): record commits it there with git's plumbing
— never a checkout, so the anchor's own checkout may be on any branch — pushes it, and opens its pull
request and makes it ready: the anchor's own merge policy lands it (its automerge, as a `/finish`'s ready
PR, or the owner's merge), D1-B's "B goes through a PR, so it survives a protected main". Not autofinish:
its sweep judges a worktree's branch only, and it would skip this one four times over — `dreams/` is
`in_place` (a gate), the receipt is JSON (not text), the dream's `➤ Answer:` lines are asks, and no
session wrote it (probed with `autofinish --dry-run` on scratch anchors, S2's landing review). `dreams/`
must be in the anchor's `in_place` (`.claude/workflow.json`): the session writes the files in the
anchor's checkout, on main (`knowledge-home init` writes it). Before D1-B the dream was committed on
the anchor's main and never pushed, which a protected main or a pre-commit that refuses main stops
outright. Once committed, the untracked working-tree copies go: an untracked file of the same path
blocks the fast-forward that brings the landed branch into the checkout (git refuses even an identical
one, probed 2026-10-31); a copy the checkout's main tracks stays.

**check** is the mechanical half of "generalised": for a confidential scope, a line that names one
of its repos (a registry key, its last segment, case-blind on word edges), a path of the scope's
(the core's `scope_match`: checkouts, worktrees, transcripts, each login's project folder, `~`
forms) or a word of the node's own list fails. The list is `<anchor>/.claude/words.txt`, one word
per line, `#` comments (at a line's start or after a space): the node's names (clients, people, products) live in the node, never here.
A line `re:<pattern>` holds an open-ended family (`re:TKT-[0-9]+`, every ticket id): a Python
regular expression, case-blind, on the same word edges, `^` and `$` per line; one that does not
compile alone, sets a flag (a global `(?i)`, or a group's `(?-i:…)`; only `(?:…)` groups), matches the
empty string or a single character (its least width under two, by re's own parser: `[a-z]`, `\\b`,
`x[0-9]*`, a bare lookahead `(?=@)`), matches plain text at all (`--`, `\\w\\w`, `[a-z]{2,}`, `\\d\\d`) or backtracks past
the clock on it, or a list that cannot be read, makes check exit 2 (and, caught when the
list is read, the scope not ready), never a pass; a hit is named by its words.txt line, never the
pattern; two lines that differ only in letter case are both kept; a `#` at a line's start or after a space starts a comment there too (elsewhere it is text); a list not in UTF-8, or a line holding a NUL or a BOM, is one that cannot be read; a pattern
spells its own whitespace (`\\s+`: a literal space matches one space, never a wrap), and its word edges
hold even where it starts or ends with punctuation (`re:-v[0-9]+` never matches `build-v2`); a pattern
that backtracks past SCOPE_PASS_SCAN_SECONDS (60) stops the scan with exit 2. A pattern's match that
is the scope's own name is exempt where the name is, so never offer the name as an alternative
(`re:node|node-x` passes `node-x` there). Write what 3.9 compiles (no possessive or atomic groups): a
list ready on one host's Python may be refused on another's. A line that began `re:` before the form
existed, a literal then, is a pattern now. The scan reads the whole text, not a line at a time: a
space in a word or a repo name matches spaces and tabs, or one hard wrap whose next line may open with a
quote or comment marker (never a blank line: no name spans paragraphs), and a hit is named by the line
it starts on. proof reports a leak its other
terms find even when a `re:` line is broken: every term but the one that fails, each pattern on its
own clock.
The anchor's own name is one, registered or not. The scope's name alone is not a hit, even where a
repo or a word shares it: the inbox file is named by it and its heading carries it, by design. A
confidential scope with no word list is not ready: an empty file (comments only) is the owner's
deliberate "none". An open scope is read whole by the global dream anyway, so check passes it. An
unreadable registry fails closed (exit 2).

**check on a brief** (confidential-reach S5, outward D4 B: the scan and a Claude judge, no person's
yes): FILE is any text about to leave the node, a seed's brief above all (`pickup seed --from`, its one
caller). The scan is the one above; a confidential scope with no word list exits 2 here too, as
`where` calls it not ready, since a scan without the node's names passes them. `--judge` then asks the
core's gate, site `scope.brief` (kind classify, provider `claude`: any other vendor needs the owner's
quote, bin/jev's confidential()), whether the text carries anything of the node the names miss — a
client's city, a domain, a figure; probe 4 of the design caught one Sonnet the scan passed. Only a
clean scan reaches it: a hit is a refusal already, and the judge never sees what the scan refused.
A missing row, an `off` one, a refused vendor or a bad reply is exit 2, never a pass. `--json`
prints {file (its base name), sha256, scope, scan, hits, judge, site, provider, model, cost_usd, time, error} — the
sha256 of the bytes judged, so a caller proves it recorded the text it sends. The object names no
term, the scope's own name included (`scope` is the core's scope_marker() of a confidential one): the
caller writes it into an open repo; the lines naming each hit go to stderr. Under --judge the scan
refuses the scope's own name too, which the pass's draft may carry (its heading does).

**record** is the export gate (Evolve S2, docs/self-improvement-scope-pass-2026-10-30.md): fence
before open (resolve() reads the registry once, fails closed), scan, judge, exact-byte receipt.
1. **The export-safe heading.** The draft's first line becomes `# Upstream — <day>`: no scope, no count
   (heading_ok, record and proof agree on it; the file's path says which scope). The text that leaves
   names no term of the node, so the scan below refuses the scope's own name as well.
2. **The scan** (check's, with the own name) over those exact bytes. A hit holds the whole draft back:
   its text moves to `<anchor>/dreams/<day>-upstream-held.md`, the vault is not touched but for the
   log line, which says `held` and nothing else, and exit 3.
3. **The judge**, a confidential scope's alone (open scopes are read whole anyway): the gate's
   `scope.upstream` row, kind classify, Claude only. `leak` holds the draft as a hit does (exit 3); a
   missing, `off` or failing judge leaves the draft where it is, logs `held` and exits 1, for a retry:
   no judgment is no export. A draft whose exact bytes already carry a `complete` receipt of the same
   judge revision is not judged again (a re-record after a failed push or commit).
4. **The receipt**, `<anchor>/dreams/<day>-receipt.json` on the records branch beside the dream: {receipt
   (complete, held or failed), scope (a confidential one's marker), day, cycle (`--cycle`, else the ISO
   week), records_ref, sha256 and bytes of the text that leaves, scan, judge, judge_rev (a hash of the
   row, the question and the labels), provider, model, cost_usd, time} — no term. A held or failed one
   never replaces a `complete` one: it goes beside it, `<day>-receipt-held.json`. The inbox file is
   written from those bytes and read back; a file whose hash differs is removed (exit 1). proof (and
   `receipt`, which weekly-run logs and the barrier reads) certifies a pass only on a `complete` receipt
   of that day, label and judge revision whose sha256 is the vault's file's, under the safe heading: a
   legacy file never certifies a new pass. A confidential file on main whose bytes no `complete`
   receipt of the day ever named (the branch's history read too) is LEAKED; one a receipt of another
   judge revision named is MISSING a current receipt.
Then the dream, the receipt (and a held file) are committed on the records branch, those paths alone,
and pushed when the anchor has an origin, its pull request opened and readied (on GitHub, with the
token git has; `SCOPE_PASS_PR` replaces that for the suite); `- <day> evolve --scope <scope>: complete|held` — a word,
never a count, as the log is pushed to the vault's main — is appended to the vault's log.md once (a
clean record after a hold the same day replaces the held line); and the inbox
file and log.md are committed through atlas's `knowledge-commit <vault-name>` (its straight form:
in_place paths on main, pushed). `SCOPE_PASS_COMMIT` replaces that command for the suite. A session
runs record as its Record step; weekly-run runs it again as a backstop when the session wrote the files
and stopped. Calling it twice is safe: a dream already on the branch is read from there.

A confidential scope's pass runs on its own yard, whose dispatch keeps its own local queue (dispatch's
per-host state dir) and reads no central path: nothing of the node waits in another host's queue.

Read-only but for record. Nothing here names a scope, path or word of the owner's: the registry is
read through the core (`common`), the vault through `weekly-run where`.
"""
import datetime as dt
import hashlib
import json
import os
import bisect
import contextlib
import re
import signal
import warnings
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path
try:   # the parser re itself uses, to find a scoped flag group anywhere in a pattern (3.11+ moved it)
    import re._parser as sre_parse
except ImportError:
    import sre_parse

HERE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(HERE.parent / "lib"))
import find_core  # noqa: E402  the regatta core's hooks/; none: one line, exit 2
sys.path.insert(0, find_core.hooks())
import common  # noqa: E402  scope_registry, scope_members, scope_match, PROJECTS, tool

PROG = "scope-pass"
SITE = "scope.brief"   # the gate's row for the judge (check --judge)
# The judge's question. Generic on purpose: the node's own names are the scan's (words.txt), and this
# text is public. What it must catch is what a word list cannot hold — a detail that identifies, data.
# Its clean list names the work's own counts, timings and decision letters (UP_ASK names counts of
# sessions or corrections): a result brief out of a confidential run is made of those (how many sessions,
# hits, seconds, which option), and before the line said so the judge read the work's figures and counts
# as the client's and refused one such brief three times (confidential-reach S1, 2026-11-04). Each clean
# item names its owner (the sessions that made the text, the scan's or fence's refusals), as a bare
# "hits", "timings" or "settings" would also clear a client's traffic, batch window or config key (the
# landing review, 2026-11-05: the bench's l12-l15). A client's figures stay a leak, however framed.
JUDGE_CONTEXT = (
    "A confidential project's session is about to send this text out: it becomes a brief committed to "
    "an open repository, read by a session that must learn nothing about the confidential project. A "
    "scan has already found none of the project's own names (its repositories, paths, clients, people "
    "and products as the project lists them). Judge what such a list misses.")
JUDGE_ASK = (
    "Does this text carry anything specific to the confidential project it comes from: a detail that "
    "identifies a client, a person or an organisation (a city, an address, a domain, an email, a company "
    "or account name, a contract, a unique description), their data or figures (revenue, prices, user "
    "counts, dates of their events, their system's internals), or a business fact about them? A generic "
    "engineering task, counts of the agent sessions, transcripts and tool calls that made this text and "
    "of the scan's or the fence's own refusals or hits, how long those sessions' steps took and when they "
    "ran, a decision's letter, the names of public tools and of their public settings, a public library "
    "or standard, a file path in the open repository and made-up example values are clean; the client's "
    "own figures, timings, dates and settings (their revenue, prices, count of users, accounts, customers "
    "or patients, their traffic, their schedules, their system's internals) are not, however the text "
    "frames them.")
JUDGE_LABELS = {
    "clean": "nothing in it identifies or describes the confidential project, its clients, people or data",
    "leak": "it carries something specific to the confidential project, its clients, people or data",
}
SITE_UP = "scope.upstream"   # the gate's row for record's judge of the upstream lines
UP_CONTEXT = (
    "A confidential project's weekly review is about to send these lines out: they become an open "
    "knowledge base's inbox, read by sessions of every other project, which must learn nothing about the "
    "confidential project. Each line is meant to be a generalised lesson that holds for any project. A "
    "scan has already found none of the project's own names (its repositories, paths, clients, people "
